# v0.0.3-alpha

This release makes concrete Alloy instances easier to inspect and expands the
evidence for comparing Canonical, raw AST, TAR, and FM24 hints.

## Instance diagrams

Each available behavioral example now has an interactive SVG showing its actual
objects and ordered relations. Relation filters, selectable details, temporal
state switching, and exact-data tables support inspection. Measured text space
and obstacle-aware connection routes reduce overlap in complex models, including
`productionLineNew · inv3`. Large drawings disclose their display limits.
The renderer uses no external graph service, image model, or runtime dependency.

The [complete catalogue image audit](../instance-audit-v003.md) checks real solver
responses for all 181 invariants, every returned example and temporal state,
and both desktop and mobile widths. It records geometry and tuple-preservation
checks, screenshots, and the limits of this finite audit. See also the
[diagram reading guide](../instance-visualization.md).

The final audit passed **2,878 state/viewport checks** for **1,342 instances**
and **1,439 states**, with no geometry or tuple-preservation failures. Of these,
1,413 states contain drawable objects or values and 26 are empty. The normal
starters cover 177 invariants; four empty starters were rejected and separately
tested using an explicit `no none` learner draft. This is not a claim that empty
starters generated images or hints.

IIS archives include the renderer and hash-version its module import, application
script, and public assets. Existing deployments must install the refreshed
package; a source release does not automatically update an IIS server.

## Hint-quality evidence

The [completed Alloy4Fun comparison](../alloy4fun-comparison.md) covers 61,598
models with five deterministic arms: Canonical, Zhang–Shasha AST, TAR depth 2,
FM24 history, and FM24 history with mutation. Each arm completed the same cohort.
The [181-invariant quality report](../alloy4fun-181-hint-quality.md) separates
hint availability on incorrect submissions from measured guidance properties.
The [ten-exercise pilot](../alloy4fun-hint-quality.md) includes literal-edit
probes and separate Luna reviews, with their disagreement retained.

These are adapted baseline experiments, not exact paper replications or human
learning studies. Current portal hints use full correct pools; saved benchmark
hints use held-out submission paths. Worker configurations also differ. Neither
native hint availability nor source localization proves that an individual edit
is executable, improves semantics, or teaches a learner successfully.

## Engine and proof-boundary corrections

The release includes the canonical quantified-body display correction, owned
JVM scratch directories with worker cleanup, two documented legacy corpus-label
corrections for evaluation, and the completed guarded benchmark recovery. Original
source data and valid prior results are preserved. The
[diagnostic report](../hint-diagnostics.md) describes remaining normalization and
matching limitations and TAR validation failures.

The image audit also exposed a host `/tmp` directory-index exhaustion affecting
Chromium. Browser-audit and CI child-process scratch files now use owned build
directories. Failed attempts and the filesystem diagnosis are retained in the
audit evidence; the renderer was not changed to disguise the infrastructure failure.

The SQL production bridge now checks parsed AST identity, and the proof reports
distinguish **PROVED** model properties, **CHECKED** production correspondence,
**TESTED** finite regressions, and **TRUSTED** runtime components. The
[SQL proof boundary](../sql-injection-proof.md) applies to its frozen inputs;
this release does not claim universal SQL immunity or whole-portal formal closure.
The 24 general portal proof obligations remain open.

## Deployment and publication

The catalogue audit reports and their hashed inputs are linked above. The
published prerelease also supplies a public evidence archive, checksum, and
verification summary with the release commit and CI run links. The usual release
gates rebuild the engine and IIS package, check all dependency hashes, run the
Python and browser regressions, and check native Windows and macOS builds.
These checks do not replace acceptance testing on an actual IIS installation.

Linux/macOS startup and Windows/IIS builds remain supported. The README now
records both this release and the SQLite, upload administration, navigation,
SQL proof, and portability additions in `v0.0.2-alpha`.

Public release assets contain only allowlisted reports and verification summaries.
Private IIS packages, configured administrator credentials, OpenAI keys, and
raw benchmark responses are excluded. Credentials are supplied separately per
deployment. Bundled public source exercise data remains inspectable in the
repository; learner-interface redaction is not repository secrecy.
